WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
Each daughter molecule keeps one old strand and builds one new strand.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.940
But the two old strands point opposite ways.

3
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.400
How can one fork build both new partners?

4
00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:17.003
Keep the first film's colour map.

5
00:00:18.503 --> 00:00:21.543
Purple marks parental templates, and cyan marks new DNA.

6
00:00:22.903 --> 00:00:27.323
The parental duplex opens into a moving Y-shaped replication fork.

7
00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:33.463
Each purple strand must guide a cyan partner, but the two templates run in opposite

8
00:00:33.463 --> 00:00:34.463
directions.

9
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:39.917
Every DNA polymerase adds a nucleotide only to a 3' end.

10
00:00:41.097 --> 00:00:44.137
New DNA therefore grows 5' to 3'.

11
00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:49.117
A copying polymerase also needs that end paired to its template.

12
00:00:50.557 --> 00:00:53.137
Replicative polymerases cannot start a chain.

13
00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:56.837
Primase must first supply a short paired primer.

14
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.220
The fork moves right, exposing more purple template.

15
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.520
Which cyan strand can grow with that fork while its polymerase extends a 3' end?

16
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:17.263
On one template, the growing 3' end faces the fork.

17
00:01:18.663 --> 00:01:23.423
Polymerase follows the opening helix and lengthens one continuous leading strand.

18
00:01:24.803 --> 00:01:28.303
Its direction of synthesis stays 5' to 3'.

19
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:33.937
On the other template, the usable 3' end faces away from the fork.

20
00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:37.517
Polymerase cannot reverse its chemistry.

21
00:01:38.997 --> 00:01:43.117
Primase starts near the fork, then polymerase extends back toward the previous

22
00:01:43.117 --> 00:01:47.577
piece. As more template opens, the cycle starts again.

23
00:01:48.877 --> 00:01:50.797
These pieces are Okazaki fragments.

24
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.970
The pieces must become one strand.

25
00:01:55.310 --> 00:02:01.370
In E. coli, DNA polymerase I removes each RNA primer while replacing it with DNA.

26
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.190
DNA ligase seals the remaining nick.

27
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:11.010
The finished lagging strand is continuous, although it was built discontinuously.

28
00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.593
Several proteins keep the fork moving.

29
00:02:16.253 --> 00:02:19.093
DnaB helicase unwinds the parental duplex.

30
00:02:20.433 --> 00:02:25.393
Single-strand binding protein holds the templates apart, and gyrase relieves

31
00:02:25.393 --> 00:02:26.393
overwinding ahead.

32
00:02:28.053 --> 00:02:29.673
DnaG primase places primers.

33
00:02:30.873 --> 00:02:35.433
DNA polymerase III, held by its β clamp, performs most synthesis.

34
00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:41.027
Eukaryotes keep the same geometry with different parts and many origins.

35
00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:43.847
CMG is the helicase.

36
00:02:45.247 --> 00:02:47.247
Pol α-primase starts chains.

37
00:02:48.607 --> 00:02:53.427
The usual summary assigns most leading synthesis to Pol ε.

38
00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:59.107
It assigns most lagging synthesis to Pol δ on PCNA.

39
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.720
Names change, but the constraint does not.

40
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.640
Every fork needs a way to open DNA, start new chains, extend them, replace starter

41
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.380
material, and seal the products.

42
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.100
The text compares these jobs and the evidence behind them.

43
00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:21.693
Polymerases extend a 3' end, so one strand is built in pieces.

44
00:03:23.173 --> 00:03:25.333
Both strands still obey the same chemistry.

45
00:03:26.593 --> 00:03:30.833
The enzyme never changes synthesis direction to solve the geometry.

46
00:03:32.293 --> 00:03:36.033
This constraint turns opposite templates into different building patterns.
